        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What speed on the treadmill is a 10-minute mile?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              6.0 mph, exactly. That is about 9.7 km/h on a metric console. At 6.0 mph you cover a tenth of a mile every minute, so the distance readout should show 1.00 mile after 10 minutes.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed on a treadmill is a 9-minute mile?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              6.67 mph. Most consoles adjust in 0.1 mph steps, so set 6.7 mph, which gives an 8:57 mile. 6.6 mph gives about a 9:05 mile. On a km/h console, 10.7 km/h is very close to a 9:00 mile.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed on a treadmill is an 8-minute mile?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              7.5 mph, which is about 12.1 km/h. Because 8 divides evenly into 60, any treadmill can hit an 8-minute mile exactly. Holding 7.5 mph gives a 5K of about 24:51 and a 10K of about 49:43.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What speed should I run on a treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Set it by effort. Easy runs, which should be most of your running, are at a speed where you can talk in full sentences; for many beginners that is 4.5 to 6 mph. Harder sessions go faster for shorter periods. Find your easy speed by raising the belt 0.2 mph a minute until talking starts to get difficult.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a 10-minute mile good for a beginner?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Running a continuous mile at 10:00 is a solid milestone for a new runner, and many experienced runners use a similar pace for easy runs. If 6.0 mph feels hard, run slower: 12- or 13-minute miles are completely normal while you build up.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Should I put the treadmill on 1% incline for a 10-minute mile?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It is optional. The 1% advice comes from a 1996 study by Jones and Doust in the Journal of Sports Sciences, which tested speeds of roughly 6.5 mph and up. At 6.0 mph air resistance is small, so a flat belt is fine; 1% is a harmless habit if you want treadmill paces to feel closer to outdoor ones.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How long does a 5K take at a 10-minute mile pace?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              About 31 minutes 4 seconds, since a 5K is 3.107 miles. A 10K at the same pace takes about 1 hour 2 minutes. These are arithmetic totals for holding 6.0 mph the whole way.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
